#{[{:activity/expected-seconds 420.0 :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186213833} :activity/id #uuid "251bed00-5af3-5da2-8724-ccbb6ef78316" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048134 :topic/id #uuid "8238e088-d8cf-5b16-b7de-d1a376704040" :topic/math-academy-id 6092} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186213833 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186214213 :tutorial/content "Let's recall what we learned in a previous lesson about linear equations with infinitely many solutions. \n\nSuppose we have the linear equation\n\n$$\n2x + 5 = 2x + 5.\n$$\n\nNotice that\n\n- the coefficient of $x$ on both sides of the equation is $2,$ and\n\n- the constant term on both sides of the equation is $5.$\n\nSince the coefficients of $x$ on both sides of the equation are equal, and the constants are also equal, it follows that this equation has *infinitely many solutions.* In fact, this equation is true for *any* value of $x.$\n\nMore generally, suppose we have the equation\n\n$$\nax + b = cx + d.\n$$\n\nIf $a = c$ and $b = d$ are *both* true, then the equation is true for any value of $x.$\n\nThe goal of this lesson is to extend this idea to quadratic polynomials." :tutorial/id #uuid "93d3fd0c-7714-59b4-970d-29dc3c5d7545" :tutorial/math-academy-id 13405 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "5d954cce-ceac-584d-81b8-41621685f324" :step/math-academy-id 476435 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186213834}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186213834 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186214223 :tutorial/content "Suppose we have two quadratic polynomials $P$ and $Q,$ given by\n\n$$\nP(x) = ax^2 + bx + c \\quad \\text{and} \\quad Q(x) = dx^2 + ex + f.\n$$\n\nIf these polynomials are equal for *all* values of $x,$ we say that they're **identical.** This means their matching terms must be the same. In other words, we must have\n\n$$\na = d, \\qquad b = e, \\qquad c = f.\n$$\n\nFor example, suppose we are given the equation\n\n$$\nax^2 + bx + c = x^2 + 5x + 4\n$$\n\nwhere $a, b,$ and $c$ are fixed numbers, and we're told that this equation is true for all values of $x.$\n\nSince this polynomial equation is true for all values of $x$, we can match the coefficients.\n\n- The coefficient of $x^2$ on the left-hand side is $a$, and on the right-hand side it is $1.$\nSo, we have\n\n$$\na = 1.\n$$\n\n- The coefficient of $x$ on the left-hand side is $b$, and on the right-hand side it is $5.$\nSo, we have\n\n$$\nb = 5.\n$$\n\n- The constant on the left-hand side is $c$, and on the right-hand side it is $4.$\nSo, we have\n\n$$\nc = 4.\n$$\n\nThis process is known as **equating coefficients. **  It’s a useful tool whenever we know two polynomials are equal for all values of the variable.\n\nThis idea works for higher-degree polynomials too: when two polynomials are equal for all $x$, we can compare the coefficients of each power of $x$ and set them equal to each other." :tutorial/id #uuid "ce7ce256-8fad-563a-81d9-48084c6f798e" :tutorial/math-academy-id 13433 :tutorial/title "Equating Coefficients of Quadratic Polynomials"} :step/id #uuid "69793601-905b-53e3-a371-2d7a2880b2ec" :step/math-academy-id 476436 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186213835}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186213835 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186213003 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186212754 :question/id #uuid "343c42bc-2a8b-5979-9a21-accb89835444" :question/math-academy-id "e-18859"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "5cf64df7-0abb-5b42-bed6-b3664c2de89a" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049310}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186213437} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186213438} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186297171} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186297180} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186297185} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186297186} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186297187}] :knowledge-point/title "Matching a Single Coefficient"} :step/id #uuid "738997c9-869e-5314-a969-85caff7c6928" :step/math-academy-id 476437 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186213836}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186213836 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186213004 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186212755 :question/id #uuid "0538ef73-5254-5d99-b709-b08c9ff89c11" :question/math-academy-id "e-18860"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "b2caeb2c-39cf-595a-8105-67d11bf0e45e" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186213439} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186213440} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186297188} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186297189} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186297190} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186297191} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186297192}] :knowledge-point/title "Solving for Two Coefficients"} :step/id #uuid "11885930-6dfe-5dec-bf2e-868ee09bcb73" :step/math-academy-id 476440 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186213837}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186213837 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186213005 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186212756 :question/id #uuid "802b17c9-3c7d-58b9-b7ee-8632f4f1cd13" :question/math-academy-id "e-18861"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "46822f28-8863-5150-a420-09c61a5c28b1" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050818} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186051017}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186213441} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186213442} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186297193} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186297210} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186297211} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186512877}] :knowledge-point/title "Comparing Factored Forms"} :step/id #uuid "5555353e-7091-5f3a-a5a5-2e6f6a73e504" :step/math-academy-id 476443}] :activity/title "Equating Polynomial Coefficients" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186213259}]}
